      "section": {
        "heading": "Opening",
        "offset": 42339
      },
      "content": "### Opening\n[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Bernab%C3%A9u_\\(stadium\\)&action=edit&section=3 \"Edit section: Opening\")]\nThe stadium was inaugurated on 14 December 1947 with a match between Real Madrid and the [Portuguese](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portugal \"Portugal\") side [Os Belenenses](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C.F._Os_Belenenses \"C.F. Os Belenenses\").[[19]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernab%C3%A9u_\\(stadium\\)#cite_note-19) After the preliminaries, at 15:30, referee [Pedro Escartín](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pedro_Escart%C3%ADn \"Pedro Escartín\") from the Madrid school started the match. Real Madrid striker [Sabino Barinaga](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sabino_Barinaga \"Sabino Barinaga\") scored the first goal in the 15th minute with a header. At the end of the match, Barinaga received a recognition pennant. Afterward, players and managers of both teams celebrated with a dinner. The first official match took place on 28 December 1947 on matchday 12 of La Liga against [Athletic Club](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athletic_Bilbao \"Athletic Bilbao\"), which had been postponed due to the inauguration of the stadium. The match was refereed by José Fombona Fernández from the Asturian school and ended with a 5–1 victory for _Los Blancos_. The first goal was scored in the 4th minute by Madrid midfielder [Chus Alonso](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chus_Alonso \"Chus Alonso\") after receiving a pass from Barinaga.[[20]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernab%C3%A9u_\\(stadium\\)#cite_note-20)\nThe stadium's official name at the time was _Estadio Real Madrid Club de Fútbol_ _Nuevo Estadio Chamartín_ English: New Chamartín Stadium _Chamartín_\n### 1950s\n[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Bernab%C3%A9u_\\(stadium\\)&action=edit&section=4 \"Edit section: 1950s\")]\n[![](https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Estadio_Bernabeu._Madrid_%2812117582503%29.jpg/330px-Estadio_Bernabeu._Madrid_%2812117582503%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Estadio_Bernabeu._Madrid_\\(12117582503\\).jpg)The stadium in 1955\nThe first major renovation occurred in 1955. On 19 June of that year, the stadium expanded to accommodate 125,000 spectators. Thus, the Madrid coliseum became the biggest stadium of all the participants of the newly established [European Cup](https",
